  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front will bring widespread precipitation to the area \nWednesday night into Thursday. The coldest air of the season \nwill move in behind the front for Friday into the weekend. \nHigher elevations of the mountains should begin to mix with and \nchange over to snow Thursday night. A couple of short waves will\nmove across the area Thursday night, and again Friday afternoon\nand Friday night, enhancing precipitation. Friday night into \nSaturday, gusty winds and cold air advection will create upslope\nsnow conditions, even though 850 mb temperatures are not quite \nin the ideal range. Models are not it good agreement with the\nplacement and amounts of precipitation for Friday and Saturday,\nleading to a lower confidence in the amount of snow for the\nhigher elevations. Even so, think that with the combination of \nthese events, several inches are possible. Boundary layer \ntemperatures will remain marginal for snow in the lowlands. If \nsnow does occur in those locations, ground temperatures should \nbe too warm for any accumulations.",
